But the system has a cheap motion-detector camera (the pre-attentive parallel system) that can look for the package with the right rough dimensions and color label. That camera didn't scan barcodes; it just noticed a package whose outline matched your target. Then the robotic arm swings the barcode scanner right to that package. At no point were all barcodes on the shelf scanned—just recognized by their shape for further inspection. --- So, you're right that your brain had to *process* something about every word-like blob in your peripheral vision to find your name, but it didn't *read* them in the scanner sense. It processed their low-level visual features in parallel, found a match, and directed your high-resolution gaze there. That's a very different mechanism than the full lexical recognition that occurs in the "double the" trick. The eyes don't read all words; they do a smart, preattentive filter that makes you feel like a superhero.
